About EKS and Kubernetes

10. What did you do in Kubernetes day to day?
"I deployed services with Helm, set up autoscaling, set CPU and memory requests and limits, fixed pods that were failing, and helped with cluster upgrades."

11. How did autoscaling work?
"We used HPA to add pods when CPU went above 70%. If there was no space for the new pods, Cluster Autoscaler added a new node. When traffic dropped, both scaled back down."

12. Tell me about a problem you fixed in Kubernetes.
"Once, pods kept restarting after a release. I checked with kubectl describe and saw they were OOMKilled, which means out of memory. I rolled back first, so users were not affected. Then we increased the memory limit and set the Java heap size correctly. After that it was stable."

13. How did you deploy with zero downtime?
"We used rolling updates with readiness probes, so a new pod gets traffic only when it's ready. For risky changes we used blue-green or canary deployments. In a canary, we sent 10% of traffic to the new version first and checked the errors before going to 100%."

About AWS and Terraform

14. Which AWS services did you use?
"EKS, EC2, VPC, IAM, S3, RDS, ECR, load balancers, Route 53 and CloudWatch."

15. How did you use Terraform?
"I wrote reusable modules for the VPC, EKS, RDS and IAM. We stored the state file in S3 and used DynamoDB for locking, so two people couldn't change the same infrastructure at the same time."

16. What if someone changes something manually in the AWS console?
"The next terraform plan shows the difference, which is called drift. Then we either fix the code or apply it again to go back to the correct state."

About Jenkins

17. Explain your Jenkins pipeline.
"When a developer merges code, a webhook starts Jenkins. The pipeline builds the code with Maven, runs the tests, checks code quality with SonarQube, builds a Docker image, scans it with Trivy, pushes it to ECR, and deploys it with Helm: first to dev, then staging, then production after approval."

18. How did you make releases 30% faster?
"We ran builds in Kubernetes pods instead of fixed servers, so jobs didn't wait in a queue. We cached Maven dependencies, ran some stages in parallel, and used one shared library for all services."

19. How do you keep passwords safe in Jenkins?
"We stored them in the Jenkins credentials store, so they are hidden in the logs. For AWS we used IAM roles, not access keys."

About monitoring

20. How did you fix problems 40% faster?
"We had too many alerts, so people started ignoring them. We removed the noisy ones and focused on what users actually feel, like errors and slow responses. We also added a runbook link and a Grafana dashboard link to every alert. After that, the on-call engineer knew where to start."

